WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Transcriber: Ivana Korom
Reviewer: Krystian Aparta

00:00:12.952 --> 00:00:15.644
I've been a journalist
for more than 23 years,

00:00:15.668 --> 00:00:17.873
at the "Arkansas Democrat-Gazette,"

00:00:17.897 --> 00:00:19.555
the "Pittsburgh Tribune Review"

00:00:19.579 --> 00:00:22.579
and most recently, "The Denver Post."

00:00:23.077 --> 00:00:26.418
(Applause)

00:00:26.442 --> 00:00:30.055
When I started
at "The Denver Post" in 2003,

00:00:30.079 --> 00:00:34.340
it was among the country's
10 largest newspapers,

00:00:34.364 --> 00:00:36.467
with an impressive subscriber base

00:00:36.491 --> 00:00:39.070
and nearly 300 journalists.

00:00:39.752 --> 00:00:41.910
At the time, I was in my 30s.

00:00:42.339 --> 00:00:44.126
Any ambitious journalist that age

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:46.747
aspires to work for one
of the big national papers,

00:00:46.771 --> 00:00:49.498
like "The New York Times"
or "The Wall Street Journal."

00:00:49.522 --> 00:00:51.403
But I was simply blown away

00:00:51.427 --> 00:00:54.094
by my first few weeks
at "The Denver Post,"

00:00:54.118 --> 00:00:57.102
and I thought,
"This is going to be my paper.

00:00:57.126 --> 00:00:59.456
I can make a career right here."

00:01:00.303 --> 00:01:02.318
Well, seven years passed,

00:01:02.342 --> 00:01:04.509
we were sold to a hedge fund,

00:01:04.533 --> 00:01:06.405
Alden Global Capital.

00:01:07.231 --> 00:01:08.913
Within a few years --

00:01:08.937 --> 00:01:10.103
(Laughs)

00:01:10.127 --> 00:01:11.840
(Laughter)

00:01:11.864 --> 00:01:13.291
Some of you know this story.

00:01:13.315 --> 00:01:16.013
(Laughter)

00:01:16.617 --> 00:01:18.109
Within a few years,

00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:20.688
buyouts ordered by past and present owners

00:01:20.712 --> 00:01:23.446
would reduce the newsroom by nearly half.

00:01:24.507 --> 00:01:25.816
And I understood.

00:01:26.261 --> 00:01:31.023
The rule of thumb used to be
that 80 percent of a newspaper's revenue

00:01:31.047 --> 00:01:34.426
came from pricy print ads and classifieds.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:38.133
With emerging giants like Google
and Facebook and Craigslist,

00:01:38.157 --> 00:01:40.918
those advertizing dollars
were evaporating.

00:01:40.942 --> 00:01:45.688
The entire industry was undergoing
a massive shift from print to digital.

00:01:45.712 --> 00:01:49.077
Alden's orders were to be digital first.

00:01:49.101 --> 00:01:52.823
Take advantage of blogs,
video and social media.

00:01:53.411 --> 00:01:55.419
They said that one day,

00:01:55.443 --> 00:02:00.952
the money we made online would make up
for the money we lost in print.

00:02:01.547 --> 00:02:03.147
But that day never came.

00:02:04.008 --> 00:02:07.315
In 2013, we won a Pulitzer Prize

00:02:07.339 --> 00:02:10.331
for covering the Aurora theater shooting.

00:02:10.847 --> 00:02:14.450
Alden ordered that more
journalists be cut.

00:02:15.109 --> 00:02:16.410
Again,

00:02:16.434 --> 00:02:17.656
and again,

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:18.831
and again,

00:02:18.855 --> 00:02:20.069
and again.

00:02:20.093 --> 00:02:24.442
We were forced to say goodbye
to talented, hardworking journalists

00:02:24.466 --> 00:02:26.593
we considered not just friends

00:02:26.617 --> 00:02:27.974
but family.

00:02:28.593 --> 00:02:32.450
Those of us left behind
were stretched impossibly thin,

00:02:32.474 --> 00:02:36.253
covering multiple beats
and writing rushed articles.

00:02:37.371 --> 00:02:41.255
Inside a windowless meeting room
in March of 2018,

00:02:41.279 --> 00:02:44.794
we learned that 30 more would have to go.

00:02:45.826 --> 00:02:48.847
This paper that once had 300 journalists

00:02:48.871 --> 00:02:51.561
would now have 70.

00:02:53.149 --> 00:02:54.966
And it didn't make sense.

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:58.210
Here, we'd won multiple Pulitzer Prizes.

00:02:58.234 --> 00:03:00.369
We shifted our focus
from print to digital,

00:03:00.393 --> 00:03:02.369
we hit ambitious targets

00:03:02.393 --> 00:03:06.932
and email from the brass
talked up the Post's profit margins,

00:03:06.956 --> 00:03:11.329
which industry experts pegged
at nearly 20 percent.

00:03:12.004 --> 00:03:15.948
So if our company was so successful
and so profitable,

00:03:15.972 --> 00:03:20.162
why was our newsroom getting
so much smaller and smaller?

00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:27.182
I knew that what was happening in Colorado
was happening around the country.

00:03:27.206 --> 00:03:29.143
Since 2004,

00:03:29.167 --> 00:03:32.842
nearly 1,800 newsrooms have closed.

00:03:33.596 --> 00:03:35.538
You've heard of food deserts.

00:03:35.914 --> 00:03:37.699
These are news deserts.

00:03:38.081 --> 00:03:41.025
They are communities,
often entire counties,

00:03:41.049 --> 00:03:44.674
with little to zero
news coverage whatsoever.

00:03:45.143 --> 00:03:46.603
Making matters worse,

00:03:46.627 --> 00:03:49.769
many papers have become ghost ships,

00:03:49.793 --> 00:03:51.896
pretending to sail with a newsroom

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:55.343
but really just wrapping ads
around filler copy.

00:03:55.883 --> 00:04:00.906
More and more newsrooms are being sold off
to companies like Alden.

00:04:01.328 --> 00:04:02.835
And in that meeting,

00:04:02.859 --> 00:04:05.593
their intentions
couldn't have been clearer.

00:04:06.174 --> 00:04:08.071
Harvest what you can,

00:04:08.095 --> 00:04:10.277
throw away what's left.

00:04:10.825 --> 00:04:15.450
So, working in secret
with a team of eight writers,

00:04:15.474 --> 00:04:18.188
we prepared a special
Sunday Perspective section

00:04:18.212 --> 00:04:20.433
on the importance of local news.

00:04:20.457 --> 00:04:22.895
(Laughter)

00:04:22.919 --> 00:04:25.545
The Denver rebellion
launched like a missile,

00:04:25.569 --> 00:04:27.926
and went off like a hydrogen bomb.

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:29.736
[In An Extraordinary Act Of Defiance,

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:31.914
Denver Post Urges Its Owner
To Sell The Paper]

00:04:31.938 --> 00:04:34.972
['Denver Post' Editorial Board
Publicly Calls Out Paper's Owner]

00:04:34.996 --> 00:04:37.233
[On The Denver Post,
vultures and superheroes]

00:04:37.257 --> 00:04:41.186
(Applause and cheers)

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:43.944
Clearly, we weren't alone in our outrage.

00:04:44.615 --> 00:04:48.276
But as expected, I was forced to resign.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.291
(Laughter)

00:04:50.315 --> 00:04:52.609
And a year later, nothing's changed.

00:04:52.633 --> 00:04:55.315
"The Denver Post"
is but a few lone journalists

00:04:55.339 --> 00:04:59.879
doing their admirable best
in this husk of a once-great paper.

00:05:01.411 --> 00:05:06.299
Now, at least some of you
are thinking to yourself,

00:05:06.323 --> 00:05:07.481
"So what?"

00:05:07.505 --> 00:05:08.662
Right?

00:05:08.686 --> 00:05:09.838
So what?

00:05:09.862 --> 00:05:11.878
Let this dying industry die.

00:05:11.902 --> 00:05:13.436
And I kind of get that.

00:05:13.823 --> 00:05:17.823
For one thing, the local news
has been in decline for so long

00:05:17.847 --> 00:05:19.863
that many of you may not even remember

00:05:19.887 --> 00:05:23.340
what it's like to have
a great local paper.

00:05:23.894 --> 00:05:26.783
Maybe you've seen
"Spotlight" or "The Paper,"

00:05:26.807 --> 00:05:30.747
movies that romanticize
what journalism used to be.

00:05:31.581 --> 00:05:35.129
Well, I'm not here
to be romantic or nostalgic.

00:05:35.153 --> 00:05:38.407
I'm here to warn you
that when local news dies,

00:05:38.431 --> 00:05:40.521
so does our democracy.

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:42.683
And that should concern you --

00:05:42.707 --> 00:05:49.266
(Applause and cheers)

00:05:50.589 --> 00:05:51.946
And that should concern you,

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:54.446
regardless of whether you subscribe.

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:55.620
Here's why.

00:05:56.176 --> 00:05:59.585
A democracy is a government of the people.

00:06:00.014 --> 00:06:04.490
People are the ultimate source
of power and authority.

00:06:04.919 --> 00:06:07.918
A great local newsroom acts like a mirror.

00:06:07.942 --> 00:06:11.855
Its journalists see the community
and reflect it back.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:14.672
That information is empowering.

00:06:14.696 --> 00:06:17.022
Seeing, knowing, understanding --

00:06:17.046 --> 00:06:19.236
this is how good decisions are made.

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:22.093
When you have a great local paper,

00:06:22.117 --> 00:06:25.814
you have journalists sitting in
on every city council meeting.

00:06:26.198 --> 00:06:29.746
Listening in to state house
and senate hearings.

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:32.016
Those important but, let's face it,

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:35.090
sometimes devastatingly boring
committee hearings.

00:06:35.114 --> 00:06:36.121
(Laughter)

00:06:36.145 --> 00:06:39.876
Journalists discover the flaws
and ill-conceived measures

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:43.788
and those bills fail,
because the public was well-informed.

00:06:43.812 --> 00:06:45.757
Readers go to the polls

00:06:45.781 --> 00:06:49.314
and they know the pros and cons
behind every ballot measure,

00:06:49.338 --> 00:06:52.290
because journalists
did the heavy lifting for them.

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:53.925
Even better,

00:06:53.949 --> 00:06:56.958
researchers have found
that reading a local paper

00:06:56.982 --> 00:07:01.682
can mobilize 13 percent
of nonvoters to vote.

00:07:02.133 --> 00:07:03.513
Thirteen percent.

00:07:03.537 --> 00:07:08.601
(Applause)

00:07:08.625 --> 00:07:12.426
That's the number that can change
the outcome of many elections.

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:15.109
When you don't have a great local paper,

00:07:15.133 --> 00:07:17.450
voters are left stranded at the polls,

00:07:17.474 --> 00:07:18.632
confused,

00:07:18.656 --> 00:07:23.450
trying to make their best guess
based on a paragraph of legalese.

00:07:24.069 --> 00:07:25.910
Flawed measures pass.

00:07:25.934 --> 00:07:29.633
Well-conceived but highly
technical measures fail.

00:07:29.657 --> 00:07:32.338
Voters become more partisan.

00:07:33.718 --> 00:07:36.666
Recently in Colorado, our governor's race

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:39.376
had more candidates
than anyone can remember.

00:07:39.837 --> 00:07:41.474
In years past,

00:07:41.498 --> 00:07:43.569
journalists would have thoroughly vetted,

00:07:43.593 --> 00:07:47.450
scrutinized, fact-checked,
profiled, debated

00:07:47.474 --> 00:07:49.878
every contender in the local paper.

00:07:50.585 --> 00:07:52.585
"The Denver Post" did its best.

00:07:53.046 --> 00:07:57.279
But in the place of past levels
of rigorous reporting and research,

00:07:57.303 --> 00:08:00.208
the public is increasingly
left to interpret

00:08:00.232 --> 00:08:04.025
dog-and-pony-show stump speeches
and clever campaign ads

00:08:04.049 --> 00:08:05.414
for themselves.

00:08:06.292 --> 00:08:09.228
With advertizing costing what it does,

00:08:09.252 --> 00:08:11.452
electability comes down to money.

00:08:11.879 --> 00:08:13.507
So by the end of the primaries,

00:08:13.531 --> 00:08:17.028
the only candidates left standing
were the wealthiest

00:08:17.052 --> 00:08:18.688
and best-funded.

00:08:18.712 --> 00:08:21.799
Many experienced
and praise-worthy candidates

00:08:21.823 --> 00:08:23.807
never got oxygen,

00:08:23.831 --> 00:08:26.212
because when local news declines,

00:08:26.236 --> 00:08:29.577
even big-ticket races become pay-to-play.

00:08:30.419 --> 00:08:33.443
Is it any surprise that our new governor

00:08:33.467 --> 00:08:37.315
was the candidate worth
more than 300 million dollars?

00:08:37.339 --> 00:08:41.523
Or that billionaire businessmen
like Donald Trump and Howard Schultz

00:08:41.547 --> 00:08:43.856
can seize the political stage?

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:47.840
I don't think this is what
the Founding Fathers had in mind

00:08:47.864 --> 00:08:50.190
when they talked about free
and fair elections.

00:08:50.214 --> 00:08:57.214
(Applause and cheers)

00:08:57.981 --> 00:09:03.837
Now this is exactly why we can't just rely
on the big national papers,

00:09:03.861 --> 00:09:06.686
like "The Journal"
and "The Times" and "The Post."

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:08.520
Those are tremendous papers,

00:09:08.544 --> 00:09:12.138
and we need them now,
my God, more than ever before.

00:09:12.504 --> 00:09:15.556
But there is no world
in which they could cover

00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:18.849
every election in every county
in the country.

00:09:19.246 --> 00:09:20.397
No.

00:09:20.421 --> 00:09:23.556
The newsroom best equipped
to cover your local election

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:26.387
ought to be your local newsroom.

00:09:26.411 --> 00:09:28.744
If you're lucky and still have one.

00:09:29.355 --> 00:09:31.371
When election day is over,

00:09:31.395 --> 00:09:36.347
a great local paper is still there,
waiting like a watchdog.

00:09:36.895 --> 00:09:38.649
When they're being watched,

00:09:38.673 --> 00:09:40.775
politicians have less power,

00:09:40.799 --> 00:09:43.029
police do right by the public,

00:09:43.053 --> 00:09:46.355
even massive corporations
are on their best behavior.

00:09:46.919 --> 00:09:52.248
This mechanism that for generations
has helped inform and guide us

00:09:52.272 --> 00:09:55.176
no longer functions the way it used to.

00:09:55.867 --> 00:10:00.307
You know intimately what the poisoned
national discourse feels like,

00:10:00.331 --> 00:10:04.085
what a mockery of reasoned
debate it has become.

00:10:04.109 --> 00:10:07.482
This is what happens
when local newsrooms shutter

00:10:07.506 --> 00:10:11.946
and communities across the country
go unwatched and unseen.

00:10:12.788 --> 00:10:16.184
Until we recognize
that the decline of local news

00:10:16.208 --> 00:10:19.081
has serious consequences for our society,

00:10:19.105 --> 00:10:22.216
this situation will not improve.

00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:25.974
A properly staffed
local newsroom isn't profitable,

00:10:25.998 --> 00:10:28.490
and in this age of Google and Facebook,

00:10:28.514 --> 00:10:30.132
it's not going to be.

00:10:30.688 --> 00:10:33.815
If newspapers are vital to our democracy,

00:10:33.839 --> 00:10:37.204
then we should fund them
like they're vital to our democracy.

00:10:37.228 --> 00:10:43.783
(Applause and cheers)

00:10:43.807 --> 00:10:47.871
We cannot stand by
and let our watchdogs be put down.

00:10:47.895 --> 00:10:51.053
We can't let more communities
vanish into darkness.

00:10:51.355 --> 00:10:54.164
It is time to debate
a public funding option

00:10:54.188 --> 00:10:56.458
before the fourth estate disappears,

00:10:56.482 --> 00:10:59.942
and with it, our grand
democratic experiment.

00:10:59.966 --> 00:11:02.299
We need much more than a rebellion.

00:11:02.323 --> 00:11:05.041
It is time for a revolution.

00:11:05.065 --> 00:11:06.227
Thank you.

00:11:06.251 --> 00:11:10.104
(Applause and cheers)

